}

phase2.explanation:
[[warn:IllegalMonitorStateException]] — wait() (same for notify()/notifyAll()) was called from outside a synchronized block on that object.

[[key:wait() needs to already hold the object's lock, because what it does is release that lock and pause]] — if the thread doesn't hold the lock in the first place, there's nothing for wait() to release, and Java refuses rather than doing something undefined.

The fix is making sure the call happens inside a synchronized context on the same object it's called on:

synchronized void method() {
    while (!conditionMet) {
        wait();  // now legal — this thread holds the lock
    }
    // proceed
}

This is a good moment to notice the pattern from this lesson: wait()/notify() only ever make sense paired with synchronized — you'll essentially never see one without the other.

